 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "All of this is in `src/tessel/views.py`.\n\n**Class-based view to view function**\n- `View.to_view_func(name)` (L86) is a classmethod that builds a plain `view(**kwargs)` function.\n- On each request, `view` instantiates the class (L106–107) and returns `self.run_view(**kwargs)`.\n  - If `init_every_request = False`, one shared instance is created up front (L115) and reused.\n- It copies the class attributes onto the function: `view.view_class` (L129) and `view.methods` (L133). It also copies the decorators from `View.decorators`.\n- You register it with `app.attach_route_rule(path, view_func=Cls.to_view_func(\"name\"))`.\n  - `attach_route_rule` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:606`) reads `view_func.methods` to decide which HTTP methods the rule accepts.\n\n**Method routing**\n- `VerbView` (L138) subclasses `View` and does two things:\n  - Its `__init_subclass__` (L165) scans the class and its bases for lowercase `get`/`post`/etc. It uppercases the names and sets `cls.methods` unless `methods` is already defined on the class.\n  - Its `run_view` (L182) runs per request.\n    - It looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`.\n    - A `HEAD` request falls back to `get` if there is no `head`.\n    - It asserts that a handler exists.\n    - It calls the handler via `active_app.ensure_sync(meth)(**kwargs)`, so `async` handlers also work.",
